            <ArticleTitle>Wellens' syndrome can indicate high-grade LAD stenosis in case of left bundle branch block.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>57-59</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00399-017-0489-z</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Diagnosing acute myocardial infarction (AMI) in left bundle branch block (LBBB) is challenging. Modified Sgarbossa criteria are known to help detect AMI in LBBB. This is a report about an electrocardiogram (ECG) with Wellens' signs in combination with a pre-existing LBBB. The ECG of a patient with fluctuating chest pain showed very subtle and one day later more obvious Wellens'signs. A left anterior descending artery (LAD) stenosis was diagnosed and successfully treated. Wellens' syndrome can be diagnosed in a case of LBBB and help detect a high-grade LAD stenosis even if modified Sgarbossa criteria are not met.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
